#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063940 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "b11d3606-7700-5c0f-89da-26b22a0ffd9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-228" :question/problem "A  $15\\,\\textrm{ft}$ ladder is leaning against a wall. If the top of the ladder is sliding down at a rate of $1\\textrm{ ft}/\\textrm{s},$ how fast is the bottom of the ladder moving away from the wall when the top of the ladder is $9\\,\\textrm{ft}$ from the ground?" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/9e0fc4dc0e9ed182c4bfdcd3b127f9343fa6030be8c549c77880c6998a265c8b.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the bottom of the ladder,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the top of the ladder and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the length of the ladder.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}.$ \n\nWe know that $y = 9\\,\\textrm{ft}$ and $z=15\\,\\textrm{ft}.$ We can find $x$ using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{9}^{2} & ={15}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =225-81 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =144 \\\\ x & =12\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = -1\\,\\textrm{ft}/\\textrm{s},$\n\n- Note that the length of the ladder remains the same, so $\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t} = 0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of $x^2+y^2=z^2$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(12)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(9)(-1) & =2(15)(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{18}{24} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ladder is sliding away at a rate of $0.75\\text{ ft}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063941 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "0284ca7f-ee39-5b9f-87f7-df109aa2200d" :question/math-academy-id "e-229" :question/problem "A rocket is launched vertically and is being tracked by a radar station which is located on the ground $8\\,\\textrm{km}$ from the launch site. If the speed of the rocket is $1700\\,\\textrm{km}/\\textrm{h}$ when it is $15\\,\\textrm{km}$ from the ground, how fast is the distance between the radar station and the rocket increasing?" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/8c28dc5302e1f6e8e6ed905737b1487b7a2c6fcea7603c75eecca037ab64ebd1.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the radar station and the launch site,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the rocket and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the radar station and the rocket.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t}.$\n\nWe know that $y = 15\\,\\textrm{km}$ and $x=8\\,\\textrm{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {8}^{2}+{15}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 64+225 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 289 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =17\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = 1700\\,\\textrm{km}/\\textrm{h}$ when $y=15,$\n\n- the distance between the radar station and the launch site ($x$) remains the same, so $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of $x^2+y^2=z^2$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(8)(0)+2(15)(1700) & =2(17)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 51000 & =34\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =1500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the radar station and the rocket is increasing at a rate of $1500\\,\\textrm{km}/\\textrm{h}$ when the rocket is $15\\,\\textrm{km}$ from the ground."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351435 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cc1cb6b-e6e3-5bb7-89da-2d643b0efb2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351437} {:answer/id #uuid "55af6bd6-2482-5862-b1d2-858b92e04e04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351438} {:answer/id #uuid "14ee404a-307f-5ba7-9321-4a08049d2319" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351439} {:answer/id #uuid "e5615bbb-907e-55da-be71-8d0c8b68c0fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351440} {:answer/id #uuid "ba77294a-ea2b-5f77-bdd3-8bd7422d2333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cc1cb6b-e6e3-5bb7-89da-2d643b0efb2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351437} :answer-field/id #uuid "1419989e-abb4-572c-8291-f4575df1dbb4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "861245ce-e152-550e-a0d5-a98fa38203e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-103781" :question/problem "A hot air balloon that is launched vertically is observed by a child located on the ground $4\\,\\text{km}$ from the launch site. What is the vertical speed of the balloon at the instant when its distance from the child is $5\\,\\text{km}$ and this distance is increasing at a rate of $12\\,\\text{km/h}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/40957ce27304474263d1d231a3a0df93193ac0461d8a7444cde3b12047d1a214.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the child and the launch site,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the launch site and the balloon,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the child and the balloon.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $z=5\\,\\text{km}$ and $x=4\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $y$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {4}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={5}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{2} & =9 \\\\ y & =3\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=12\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- the distance between the child and the launch site ($x$) remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(4)(0)+2(3)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(5)(12) \\\\ 6\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =120 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{120}{6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the vertical speed of the hot air balloon is $20\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a627611e-1de8-5fee-a092-69f8b0d865e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "61\\text{ km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351444} {:answer/id #uuid "68d975c6-e233-54e6-afa7-cbfd38eba4ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65.5\\text{ km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351445} {:answer/id #uuid "30c44124-e9d8-5878-8bf7-f331ec526790" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\text{ km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351446} {:answer/id #uuid "64c1febb-03b2-55d4-a34c-0e6dd53133ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70\\text{ km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351447} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f79fc6-ff1e-57c4-89ff-0c38e9a02ed4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75.2\\text{ km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64c1febb-03b2-55d4-a34c-0e6dd53133ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70\\text{ km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351447} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4ccba75-b5f2-5edd-b43f-3b682b4639a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351443}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "507fbe2b-2f95-5bfa-be32-3403810daadf" :question/math-academy-id "q-2939" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/9283bcc77ac8e63c1ba00f9a81e659bcbcf41bc98b94a1a0617b02feab71cf0a.png)\n\nCar $B$ is being driven due south toward Caltech at a speed of $60\\,\\text{km/h},$ while car $A$ is being driven due east away from Caltech. When car $B$ is $0.6\\,\\text{km}$ away from Caltech, car $A$ is $0.8\\,\\text{km}$ from Caltech. If the distance between the two vehicles is increasing at a rate of $20\\,\\text{km/h},$ what is the speed of car $A?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/6e7ef0d4665710a31c1c601048498f20ad6b3099769c1d140320074805964ec7.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Caltech and car $A$,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between Caltech and car $B$,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the two cars.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=0.8\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=0.6\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {0.8}^{2}+{0.6}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1 \\\\ z & =1\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-60\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=20\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(0.8)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(0.6)(-60) & =2(1)(20) \\\\ 1.6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =40+72 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{112}{1.6} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =70.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the car $A$ is $70\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4739f617-728e-5b4a-98a7-1bfb1f5e6d1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.2\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351451} {:answer/id #uuid "94acd51e-40d6-56a4-bf4a-c5cf4a3ea6f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.2\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351452} {:answer/id #uuid "74332d96-fbda-56d8-ac95-5a2e5da09491" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351453} {:answer/id #uuid "9079fd88-c4c7-53a5-9b1c-8c0bb299341b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351454} {:answer/id #uuid "efc3f97c-296b-5986-b62c-9eaf4c0ca903" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.8\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94acd51e-40d6-56a4-bf4a-c5cf4a3ea6f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.2\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351452} :answer-field/id #uuid "24d1fc14-9f68-5961-b4c9-1d96d16ba126" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351450}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a5414ba0-25e4-59ae-971e-c242926e55b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-30080" :question/problem "Two cars, $A$ and $B,$ are both initially at the point $O,$ and they both depart from $O$ at the same time. Car $A$ travels due east, and its distance from $O$ is given by $3t\\,\\text{ft}.$ Car $B$ travels due north, and its distance from $O$ is $2{t}^{2}\\,\\text{ft}.$ Here, $t$ is the time, measured in seconds. What is the rate of the change of the distance between the two cars after $2$ seconds?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/aa3f39f65b0785cfca2d1545e4bf331281f523f5e19777372dbb44096bdb4223.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between car $A$ and point $O$,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between car $B$ and point $O$, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between car $A$ and car $B.$\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the distance traveled by the cars after $2$ seconds. We get \n\n$$\nx=x(2)=3(2)=6\\text{ ft}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=y(2)=2(2)^{2}=8\\text{ ft}.\n$$\n\nWe can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {6}^{2}+{8}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 100 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =10\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe velocity of the cars is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(t)=3$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{′}(t)=4t.$ So, when $t=2$ their velocities are\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={y}^{′}(2)=8\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds,\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(2)=3\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds.\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(6)(3)+2(8)(8) & =2(10)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 164 & =20\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =8.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the cars is changing at a rate of  $8.2\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a031a04-22f7-576a-bc5e-5f5e48c44e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351458}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a031a04-22f7-576a-bc5e-5f5e48c44e1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351458} :answer-field/id #uuid "abd7da65-7704-5e74-b0ab-ab4ad4d4e595" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351457}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d23bc7a2-a66d-5a97-8b57-920fdc962162" :question/math-academy-id "q-313982" :question/problem "A $15\\text{ ft}$ painter’s plank leans against a wall. The bottom end slides horizontally away from the wall at a constant rate of $3\\text{ ft/s}.$ How fast is the top end sliding down the wall at the instant when it is $9\\text{ ft}$ above the ground? Assume the rate at which the top slides downward is **positive**.\n\nThe top end is sliding down at a speed of \n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/afdb53c3134f18add3b07400805c76a36f3f7a6b8265a3f32e9e0dfaf1dd36e9.png)\n\nIn the diagram, \n\n- $x$ is the distance between the bottom of the plank and the wall,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the top of the plank and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the length of the plank.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $y=9\\,\\text{ft}$ and $z=15\\,\\text{ft}.$ We can find $x$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{9}^{2} & ={15}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =225-81 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =144 \\\\ x & =12\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$\n\n- The length of the plank remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(12)(3)+2(9)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(15)(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{72}{18} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the top end of the plank is sliding down at a speed of $4\\,\\text{ft/s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95b3a29e-a085-5c5d-bd80-8430b253507e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95b3a29e-a085-5c5d-bd80-8430b253507e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351461} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5feedf8-aa34-5525-8c88-e94f493c76c5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351460}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca2614df-5f7b-5ee7-bf5b-fa3d6af53b32" :question/math-academy-id "q-344498" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/c47df1761c64d428216092502779c45048dc68b98d8ecbc1f483aac484b0b67b.png)\n\nA car $B$ is driving due south toward an intersection at a speed of $60\\,\\text{km/h},$ while car $A$ is driving due east away from the intersection. When car $B$ is $12\\,\\text{km}$ from the intersection, car $A$ is $9\\,\\text{km}$ from the intersection. If the distance between the two cars is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{km/h},$ what is the speed of car $A?$\n\nThe speed of car $A$ is \n{{field-1}}$\\text{ }\\text{km/h}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/a89093949e721fffc50a2c8905ec8e081d45ce87e7ed3178a21121847dd67faf.png)\n\nIn the diagram:\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the intersection and car $A$\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the intersection and car $B$\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the two cars\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe know $x=9\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=12\\,\\text{km}.$ First find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {9}^{2}+{12}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 81+144 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =225 \\\\ z & =15\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that: \n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-60\\,\\text{km/h}$ (toward the intersection)\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=6\\,\\text{km/h}$ (increasing distance between cars)\n\nDifferentiate ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and substitute the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(9)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(12)(-60) & =2(15)(6) \\\\ 18\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}-1440 & =180 \\\\ 18\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =1620 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =90\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of car $A$ is $90\\,\\text{km/h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "24df65be-5d7c-5943-b7ad-b330fb8890c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351464} {:answer/id #uuid "3fcbe045-2c3a-591a-b7d2-22485d99bf84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351465}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24df65be-5d7c-5943-b7ad-b330fb8890c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351464} :answer-field/id #uuid "30e66efb-fe7f-56f9-8dde-0d6c44cec7df" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c69063ca-2272-5fce-976c-d5f9e734d3d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-346032" :question/problem "Two snowplows are approaching a maintenance station $T$ along perpendicular roads. Snowplow $E$ drives due south toward $T$ at $24\\,\\text{mph},$ while snowplow $F$ drives due west toward $T$ at $10\\,\\text{mph}.$ When snowplow $F$ is $6\\,\\text{miles}$ from $T$ and snowplow $E$ is $8\\,\\text{miles}$ from $T$, how fast is the distance between the snowplows decreasing? Round your answer to the **nearest integer** and assume that the rate of decreasing distance is **positive**.\n\nThe speed of convergence of the two snowplows is approximately\n{{field-1}} $\\text{mph}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/1109f4736ddfa81344d545802438588967aa18aac379c75a6b7be58688fb931f.png)\n\nIn the diagram:\n\n- $x$ is the distance between snowplow $F$ and the maintenance station $T$\n\n- $y$ is the distance between snowplow $E$ and the maintenance station $T$\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the snowplows.\n\nBoth snowplows are moving toward $T$, so the leg rates are negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{dt} & =-10\\text{ }\\text{mph} \\\\ \\frac{dy}{dt} & =-24\\text{ }\\text{mph}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, compute $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {6}^{2}+{8}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 36+64 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =100 \\\\ z & =10\\text{ }\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiate ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and substitute the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x\\frac{dx}{dt}+2y\\frac{dy}{dt} & =2z\\frac{dz}{dt} \\\\ 2(6)(-10)+2(8)(-24) & =2(10)\\frac{dz}{dt} \\\\ -120-384 & =20\\frac{dz}{dt} \\\\ -504 & =20\\frac{dz}{dt} \\\\ \\frac{dz}{dt} & ≈-25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer.\n\nSo, the speed of convergence of the two snowplows is approximately $25\\text{ }\\text{mph}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94c5ef8a-5d90-5fab-be39-5d5a202da549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94c5ef8a-5d90-5fab-be39-5d5a202da549" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351468} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8cf9468-e5bc-565f-bdf9-40d5ded0b431" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f7c0283a-ec29-50bd-a4bf-7be6d57943ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-346050" :question/problem "A surveyor stands $15\\text{ ft}$ away from the base of a radio tower. An elevator inside the tower moves straight up at a constant speed of $20\\text{ ft/s}.$ How fast is the distance between the surveyor and the elevator changing when the elevator is $20\\text{ ft}$ above the ground?\n\nThe distance between the surveyor and the elevator is\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/7a6f5b05bc0bd89d01b1b521fa35f4bc613e532a06ce04fea26323e6c5dbbafa.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the surveyor and the base of the tower,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the elevator and the ground,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the surveyor and the elevator.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=15\\,\\text{ft}$ and $y=20\\,\\text{ft}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {15}^{2}+{20}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 625 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =25\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=20\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$\n\n- the distance between the surveyor and the base of the tower remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(15)(0)+2(20)(20) & =2(25)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 800 & =50\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the surveyor and the elevator is $16\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f4415ae7-8ebc-524a-b7f4-f6b5bef8bbad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,\\text{ mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351471} {:answer/id #uuid "501b1fcd-d219-53bc-899a-fd5dfa6553fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96\\,\\text{ mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351472} {:answer/id #uuid "00ad3447-2192-57b6-887f-85dd9193168a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144\\,\\text{ mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351473} {:answer/id #uuid "08751a96-8570-54c3-8e54-e0f1ac5669df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\,\\text{ mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351474} {:answer/id #uuid "979e5140-bd9f-5472-ae49-8d2a366b3752" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\,\\text{ mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08751a96-8570-54c3-8e54-e0f1ac5669df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\,\\text{ mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351474} :answer-field/id #uuid "311a83f5-4507-59e0-b813-3205814f9e31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0619c060-6164-5592-9cc0-f1974f0934ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-48953" :question/problem "Two buses, $A$ and $B,$ drive toward a school. Bus $A$ travels south at a constant velocity of $30\\,\\text{ mph},$ while the bus $B$ travels west at a constant velocity of $40\\,\\text{mph}.$ If bus $A$ is $16$ miles from the school, and bus $B$ is $12$ miles from the school, how fast is the distance between the buses decreasing?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram of the situation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/07eb0e89a4dc85160deb45ee13e6189d32bbc905bc638cb1aee891f0504ca950.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between bus $A$ and school,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between bus $B$ and school,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the two buses.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=12\\,\\text{mi}$ and $y=16\\,\\text{mi}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =(12)^{2}+(16)^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =400 \\\\ z & =20\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-40\\,\\text{mph},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-30\\,\\text{mph}.$\n\nNote that the rates of change are both negative because the distance to the school is decreasing for both buses. \n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(12)(-40)+2(16)(-30) & =2(20)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 40\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-960-960 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1920}{40} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the buses is decreasing at a rate of $48\\,\\text{mph}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ebdb727b-9eac-5596-b394-af4a4b24af2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351478} {:answer/id #uuid "8c81c336-2844-55b8-8da7-a3af65b90ba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351479} {:answer/id #uuid "b77fb926-7f27-5c11-8518-693d9926a170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351480} {:answer/id #uuid "d5299219-5c17-551b-81f9-0d5fbb1832c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351481} {:answer/id #uuid "77dc31ff-775c-54c6-ad62-819d139d7aaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77dc31ff-775c-54c6-ad62-819d139d7aaa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351482} :answer-field/id #uuid "0c9da5ee-3602-51df-be37-f5ab5e2ee3c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a77e15ea-ab18-5b8f-bc23-048daefb79b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-49007" :question/problem "A drone is released vertically from the ground at a constant velocity of $40\\text{ ft/s}.$ An observer stands $120\\text{ ft}$ away from the launch point. What is the rate of change of the distance between the drone and the observer $4$ seconds after the drone was launched?" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/3f85af53794fa2f61a353f2e088cc1cecc9fc04f7453b97aa0ca808475e40035.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the observer and the launch point,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the drone and the launch point, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the observer and the drone.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the drone altitude after $4$ seconds. Since its velocity is constant, we get \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot t=40(4)=160\\text{ ft}\n$$\n\nWe can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {120}^{2}+{160}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 40\\,000 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =200\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=40\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$\n\n- the distance between the observer and the launch pad remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(120)(0)+2(160)(40) & =2(200)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 12\\,800 & =400\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the drone and the observer increases at a rate of $32\\,\\text{ft/s}$ after $4$ seconds."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d8b8820-1931-5088-ada3-0a64df6c20c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.25\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351485} {:answer/id #uuid "6d7d34de-f69c-5941-9ad5-b48b4e5087f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.7\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351486} {:answer/id #uuid "edef131c-1a41-5809-9ba5-2388b4617cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.6\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351487} {:answer/id #uuid "85048623-d94d-5fa8-9887-cbad1bb7fa0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.3\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351488} {:answer/id #uuid "1ae48c39-8212-58c1-a7c1-a4d99af765b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.85\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d7d34de-f69c-5941-9ad5-b48b4e5087f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.7\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351486} :answer-field/id #uuid "1dfd7748-4998-503e-a253-120bc977aca7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ea871d90-c900-55e4-a241-c287fbf062e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-49008" :question/problem "Ivan and Elisabeth have a date at the mall.\nIvan arrived at the agreed time, but Elisabeth is late. After $30$ minutes of waiting, Ivan decides to go home.\n\nIvan travels north from the mall with a speed of $4\\text{km/h}.$ Elisabeth travels west toward the mall. When Ivan is $1.2\\,\\text{km}$ from the mall, Elisabeth is $0.5\\,\\text{km}$ from the mall. If the distance between them increases at a speed of $1.5\\,\\text{km/h},$ what is Elisabeth's speed?" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14066033/assets/2ddd52ba5c32fd8fa9b1a24ec9770bfda5103f61d365c49c13c1f52fbaa799bd.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Elisabeth and the mall,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between Ivan and the mall,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between Ivan and Elisabeth.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=0.5\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=1.2\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {0.5}^{2}+{1.2}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =1.69 \\\\ z & =1.3\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=4\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=1.5\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(0.5)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}+2(1.2)(4) & =2(1.3)(1.5) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =3.9-9.6 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5.7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Elisabeth is approaching the mall with a speed of $5.7\\,\\text{km/h}.$"}]}
